  "summary": "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Let $\\mathcal{I} \\to \\Sh(\\mathcal{C})$, $i \\mapsto \\mathcal{F}_i$ be a filtered diagram of sheaves of sets.…",
  "detail": "Let $\\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Let $\\mathcal{I} \\to \\Sh(\\mathcal{C})$, $i \\mapsto \\mathcal{F}_i$ be a filtered diagram of sheaves of sets. Consider the canonical map $$ \\Psi : \\colim_i \\Gamma(\\mathcal{C}, \\mathcal{F}_i) \\longrightarrow \\Gamma(\\mathcal{C}, \\colim_i \\mathcal{F}_i) $$ We have the following: If all the transition maps are injective then $\\Psi$ is injective. If $\\Sh(\\mathcal{C})$ is quasi-compact, then $\\Psi$ is injective. If $\\Sh(\\mathcal{C})$ is quasi-compact and all the transition maps are injective then $\\Psi$ is an isomorphism. Assume there exists a set $S \\subset \\Ob(\\Sh(\\mathcal{C}))$ with the following properties: for every surjection $\\mathcal{F} \\to *$ there exists a $\\mathcal{K} \\in S$ and a map $\\mathcal{K} \\to \\mathcal{F}$ such that $\\mathcal{K} \\to *$ is surjective, for $\\mathcal{K} \\in S$ the product $\\mathcal{K} \\times \\mathcal{K}$ is quasi-compact. Then $\\Psi$ is bijective.",
