  "summary": "Let $\\mathcal{I}$, $\\mathcal{J}$ be index categories. Let $M : \\mathcal{I} \\times \\mathcal{J} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be a functor.…",
  "detail": "Let $\\mathcal{I}$, $\\mathcal{J}$ be index categories. Let $M : \\mathcal{I} \\times \\mathcal{J} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ be a functor. Assume that $M_{i, \\infty} = \\colim_j M_{i,j}$ exists for all $i$. Then the resulting functor $M_{-, \\infty} : \\mathcal{I} \\to \\mathcal{C}$ has a colimit if and only if $M$ does, and then the colimits coincide. In particular, we have $$ \\colim_i \\colim_j M_{i, j} = \\colim_{i, j} M_{i, j} = \\colim_j \\colim_i M_{i, j} $$ provided all the indicated colimits exist. Similar for limits.",
