  "family": "map",
  "summary": "Let $f : X \\to S$ be a morphism of schemes. Let $x \\in X$ be a point. Set $s = f(x)$. Let $X_s$ be the fibre of $f$ at $s$.…",
  "detail": "Let $f : X \\to S$ be a morphism of schemes. Let $x \\in X$ be a point. Set $s = f(x)$. Let $X_s$ be the fibre of $f$ at $s$. Assume $f$ is locally of finite type. The following are equivalent: The morphism $f$ is quasi-finite at $x$. The point $x$ is isolated in $X_s$. The point $x$ is closed in $X_s$ and there is no point $x' \\in X_s$, $x' \\not = x$ which specializes to $x$. For any pair of affine opens $\\Spec(A) = U \\subset X$, $\\Spec(R) = V \\subset S$ with $f(U) \\subset V$ and $x \\in U$ corresponding to $\\mathfrak q \\subset A$ the ring map $R \\to A$ is quasi-finite at $\\mathfrak q$.",
