  "detail": "Let $f : X \\to Y$ be a continuous map of topological spaces. Let $(\\mathcal{C}, F)$ be a type of algebraic structures. Let $\\mathcal{F}$ be a sheaf with values in $\\mathcal{C}$ on $X$. Let $\\mathcal{G}$ be a sheaf with values in $\\mathcal{C}$ on $Y$. Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis for the topology on $Y$. Suppose given for every $V \\in \\mathcal{B}$ a morphism $$ \\varphi_V : \\mathcal{G}(V) \\longrightarrow \\mathcal{F}(f^{-1}V) $$ of $\\mathcal{C}$ compatible with restriction mappings. Then there is a unique $f$-map (see Definition \\ref{definition-f-map} and discussion of $f$-maps in Section \\ref{section-presheaves-structures-functorial}) $\\varphi : \\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{F}$ recovering $\\varphi_V$ for $V \\in \\mathcal{B}$.",
